.spec/decisions.md-26-| ADR-022 | Proposed | Require an ordered, explicit per-profile `dnsResolver` containing a non-empty list of numeric IPv4/IPv6 DNS endpoints; select generation-scoped DNS-over-TCP through authenticated SSH for M1 and the same endpoints for M2 relay UDP with explicitly bounded TCP retry. There is no Relux/product resolver default, exit-host discovery, hostname resolver endpoint, tunneled DoH, or physical-resolver fallback in the baseline. TASK-260715-1tnjlu owns schema and failure invariants. TASK-260721-3miqh4 proves the exact 65,535-byte DNS / 65,537-byte framed boundary, structural attempts, corrected accounting equations, 20 validator-backed default/hard timing mutations, exact reliability traces, and observed cleanup. Its 4-endpoint/16-owner/4 MiB/35-second candidate default and 8-endpoint/32-owner/8 MiB/135-second candidate envelope are not production-authorized: policy verification requires the exact non-authoritative class, false authorization booleans, blocker IDs, later physical gate, and structural contracts. ADR-014 has no selected-SSH `direct-tcpip` evidence and ADR-009 has no accepted residual DNS component ledger. Machine policy sets `productionAuthorization.permitted=false`; consumers remain blocked until TASK-260715-1gjxer, selected-engine controlled rows, TASK-260715-1pn983, physical provider evidence, and independent architecture review are accepted. |
.spec/decisions.md-27-
.spec/decisions.md-28-| ADR-023 | Accepted | Scope the M0 SSH transport contract to **viability** (Option A). M0-viability-mandatory: pre-auth raw host-key evidence, approved public-key authentication, `direct-tcpip`, bidirectional exec plus exec/stdin relay upload, client-initiated rekey by byte/time threshold, bounded buffers and backpressure, deterministic cancellation and lifecycle, keepalive, privacy-safe errors, and cheaply-available metrics. Deferred to M3 as explicitly evidence-gated obligations, never silent waivers: (1) consumer-driven receive-window credit with an immutable per-channel cap, (2) the RFC 4254 channel-open rejection reason taxonomy, (3) exact exec-exit metadata distinguishing `status(0)` from `notReported` and exposing `coreDumped`, (4) deep rekey and keepalive observability including server-initiated KEX lifecycle/generation and reply-correlated keepalive RTT/timeout/miss. M0 adapters MUST surface deferred semantics as explicit not-reported/unsupported states. Owner tasks: `TASK-260728-yx2fca` (contract re-scope), `TASK-260728-3cveay` (M3 delivery). Resolves the three-round `TASK-260715-1ozsb6` stop-the-line. |
.spec/decisions.md:29:| ADR-024 | Accepted | The prototype physical target is **macOS-only on the current Apple-silicon Mac**. iOS targets, provider, UI, TestFlight, and App Store work are deferred; their identifiers stay defined in the Apple identifier/entitlement matrix so they resume without renaming. Gate P0 is recorded from macOS evidence alone. A Linux CI runner and native Linux relay-asset execution evidence are **not** required for the working-client path; they move to the release path (`TASK-260715-1c4l9v`). Relux Works Apple Development and Developer ID Application **signing identities** are present in the local Keychain. Notarization credentials are **not** yet in a usable Keychain form — see ADR-025. Secrets stay in the Keychain and are never copied to repo, board, logs, or `providerConfiguration`. |
.spec/decisions.md:30:| ADR-025 | Accepted | **Notarization credential custody is Keychain-only.** The only sanctioned runtime form of the App Store Connect notarization credential is a **named `notarytool` Keychain credential profile**; only that profile *name* may be recorded on the board, in the repo, or in CI configuration. The owner-supplied source `.p8` private key currently exists as a mode-0600 file on this host, which does not satisfy the Keychain-only invariant. Before the notarization path is declared ready, `TASK-260728-dveo1o` must (a) store the named profile, (b) verify it against the real notary service, and (c) record an explicit approved disposition for the source file — migration into Keychain-backed custody, retention under a named custodian with documented access control, or secure destruction. The file path, key ID, issuer ID, and key bytes are never echoed, logged, committed, or stored in board resources, and `set -x`/shell history must not capture the `store-credentials` invocation. CI keeps its own separately-scoped credential; the local profile is not exported. |
.spec/decisions.md-31-| ADR-026 | Accepted | **Sparkle key generation is separated from updater integration.** The EdDSA keypair is generated and taken into custody at the up-front ceremony `TASK-260728-q5kjta`, because it depends on nothing; `TASK-260717-ziprhs` records the resulting public-key/custody evidence unattended. `SUPublicEDKey` pinning, CI signing-secret binding, and appcast sign/verify evidence are a distinct downstream task (`TASK-260728-3bj9bk`) blocked by the generated macOS target (`TASK-260715-uyju7n` → `TASK-260717-xempiv`) and the appcast pipeline (`TASK-260717-1mt4e7`). An accepted key ceremony never implies a working self-update signing path. |
.spec/decisions.md-32-| ADR-027 | Accepted | **Deferral is expressed as `blocked` status, not as an unlinked backlog item.** A task deferred by owner decision — Gate A0 research (`TASK-260715-1o3q6l`), further `ReluxNIOSSH` fork work (`TASK-260715-1af33i`), iOS targets (`TASK-260715-33oofa`), physical-iPhone gates (`TASK-260715-1kntdx`) — carries `blocked` status with the evidence packet naming the deciding ADR and the exact input that would resume it. Leaving a deferred task in `backlog` with its blockers removed makes it *schedulable*, which is how prohibited work re-enters an autonomous run. Retained evidence and downstream links are preserved so the branch re-arms unchanged. |
.spec/decisions.md-33-| ADR-028 | Accepted | **One human interaction is one board node, and a human node is never counted as autonomous.** (a) *Ownership.* A human sitting is owned by a single task; `TASK-260728-q5kjta` owns Ceremony C1 and is the only up-front human-input node. The evidence tasks it unblocks (`TASK-260715-apc34w`, `TASK-260715-3jloqy`, `TASK-260728-dveo1o`, `TASK-260717-ziprhs`) are agent work that runs unattended with the granted access and keeps its full evidence obligations. Spreading one sitting across several tasks forces the human to re-appear after each producer-reviewer cycle, which is what the round-2 review rejected. (b) *Labelling.* A task whose completion requires a human action or decision is a **human node** — a scheduler barrier that is never counted in an autonomous total, even when agent work follows inside the same node. Tasks that merely consume access or a decision granted earlier are autonomous and are reported as that node's follow-up. (c) *An approval that acknowledges a not-yet-existing artifact cannot be pre-granted*: Approval A1 (`TASK-260715-9yp8to`), sign-off S1 (`TASK-260715-2ayxqn` AC5), the manual ratification checkpoints (`TASK-260717-1dsqnj`, `TASK-260717-l639qp`, `TASK-260717-2d308k`), and the release-candidate approvals (`TASK-260715-1r48pc`, `TASK-260715-yynqbr`, `TASK-260715-2aessv`) are therefore separate, later interactions, not C1 content. |
